-- title:
--   The adic frame ring is local with non-zero reduction mod π
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, $K_0$ a field realised as its fraction field, and $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$. Let $C$ be a field which is a $K_0$-algebra, equipped with a valuation taking values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, and let $R$ be a commutative $\mathcal{O}$-algebra mapping to $C$, all algebra maps being compatible in the sense that $\mathcal{O} \to R \to C$ and $\mathcal{O} \to K_0 \to C$ are scalar towers. Let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformizer of $K_0$ in $C$, that is, an element $\varpi \in K_0$ with $0 < v(\varpi) < 1$ in $C$ and such that every non-zero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume `IsAdicFrame` $\pi\,\varpi\,R$: $\pi$ is irreducible; $R \to C$ is injective; its image is exactly the unit ball $\{c \in C : v(c) \le 1\}$; $R$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$; the elements $a \in K_0$ with $v(a) \le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal{O}$; the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed; and $\pi$ and $\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Then $R$ is a local ring, and `modPow` $\pi\,R\,0$, the quotient $R/(\pi^{0+1}) = R/\pi R$, is non-trivial.
--
--   This records the two elementary ring-theoretic facts about the integral frame $R$ of the formal upper half plane: $R$ is the valuation ring of $C$, hence local, and its residue reduction at $\pi$ is non-zero. Both are used when producing adic points of Čerednik–Drinfeld quotients and when comparing $R$ with its reductions `modPow`.
